.TH DISKVIEW 1 "2026-08-16" "notcoreutils" "notcoreutils Manual"
.SH NAME
diskview \- recursively size a directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B diskview
[\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-top\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-exclude\fR \fIPATTERN\fR]... [\fIDIR\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B diskview
recursively sizes DIR and prints the biggest entries first \- a
non\-interactive slice of what ncdu shows.
.PP
Formerly named \fBdv\fR - kept as an alias name, see this directory's
own \fIalias\fR file. Not to be confused with \fBdd\fR(1)/\fBdf\fR(1)/
\fBdu\fR(1) (real, unrelated classic Unix utilities kept under their
own standard names) - the similarity of the short name \fBdv\fR to
those was exactly the source of confusion this rename fixes.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-n\fR, \fB\-\-top \fIN\fR
How many biggest entries to print (default 20).
.TP
.B \-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Print each directory as it is entered during the scan.
.TP
.B \-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Suppress the final entry listing entirely.
.TP
.B \-exclude \fIPATTERN\fR
Prune any directory matching PATTERN (glob syntax) from the walk
entirely, rather than just hiding it from the listing. Repeatable.
